33929098118,"displayIndex":0,"starred":false,"workflowState":1,"authorId":"54720b16e4b0fbcd359feb23","systemDataId":"1416850790176-V8E1GIVCABZ3KE0SDMEU","systemDataVariants":"1200x1500,100w,300w,500w,750w,1000w","systemDataSourceType":"JPEG","systemDataOrigin":"USER_UPLOAD","filename":"912craftwork-001.jpg","mediaFocalPoint":{"x":0.5,"y":0.5,"source":3},"colorData":{"topLeftAverage":"010200","topRightAverage":"000001","bottomLeftAverage":"010201","bottomRightAverage":"000101","centerAverage":"010000","suggestedBgColor":"d4ac71"},"urlId":"8pz2t2i36xi2k3hps2yvudpzj2pyoh","title":"","body":"<p class=\"\" style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\">The Cumberland Street rowhouse rehab was conceived as a showcase for our work. We started with a structure from the 1880s that had seen better days; once surrounded by the thriving textile industry, the neighborhood had slid into disrepair and the building was a blighted drug house for many years. Our mission was to restore the house to a modern version of its former glory. This house describes the firm\u2019s design principles as well as our dedication to ethical and sustainable building -  using reclaimed and locally sourced materials whenever possible including reclaimed interior doors throughout the house, Heart Pine flooring from a South Philly factory building, locally milled wood, and slate tile from a PA slate quarry. The cast concrete elements (countertops, windowsills, and a sink) feature inlaid glass, stone, and antique tile. The entire home itself is a reclaimed material.</p>","location":{"mapZoom":12.0,"mapLat":40.7207559,"mapLng":-74.0007613,"markerLat":40.7207559,"markerLng":-74.0007613,"addressLine1":"","addressLine2":"","addressCountry":""},"likeCount":0,"commentCount":0,"publicCommentCount":0,"commentState":1,"unsaved":false,"fullUrl":"/project-3/8pz2t2i36xi2k3hps2yvudpzj2pyoh","assetUrl":"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/54720b17e4b05ab9931fa7bb/1416850790176-V8E1GIVCABZ3KE0SDMEU/912craftwork-001.jpg","con